---
A high-performance JavaScript and TypeScript compiler toolchain written in Zig, usable from Zig and from JavaScript through npm.

[Try it in the playground →](https://playground.yuku.fyi)

## [Why Yuku]($section.id('why-yuku'))

**Correct.** 100% ECMAScript spec compliant, with zero failures and zero AST mismatches across the [parser test suite](/testing/).

**Fast.** Data-oriented from the ground up, with a flat AST and one arena per parse.

**Small.** A fraction of the install size of the alternatives, see [Size](#size).

**Zero dependencies.** Pure Zig, easy to build, embed, and cross-compile.

**Modern.** Full support for modern and experimental syntax, including decorators, source phase and deferred imports, and `using` declarations.

## [The toolchain]($section.id('the-toolchain'))

- [Parser](/parser/): JavaScript, TypeScript, JSX, and declaration files to an AST, recovering from errors.
- [AST](/parser/ast/): a flat, data-oriented tree, with comments and tokens alongside.
- [Semantic analysis](/parser/semantic/): scopes, symbols, resolved references, every early error, and module records.
- [Traverser](/parser/traverse/): typed visitors in four modes, from plain walks to full semantics and in-place transforms.
- [Codegen](/parser/codegen/): printing back to source, with type stripping, minification, and source maps.

## [At a glance]($section.id('at-a-glance'))

Parse TypeScript, analyze it, and print it back as minified JavaScript.

```js
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";
import { analyze } from "yuku-analyzer";
import { generate } from "yuku-codegen";

const source = "export const greet = (name: string) => `Hello, ${name}`;";

generate(parse(source, { lang: "ts" }).program, { strip: true, minify: true }).code;
// "export const greet=(name)=>`Hello, ${name}`"

analyze(source, { lang: "ts" }).exports.map((e) => e.name);
// ["greet"]
```

The same pipeline in Zig.

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, source, .{ .lang = .ts });
defer tree.deinit();

const semantic = try parser.semantic.analyze(&tree);
const records = try parser.semantic.module_record.collect(&tree, &semantic);

const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{
    .strip = true,
    .minify = true,
    .format = .compact,
});
defer result.deinit(allocator);
```

## [Install]($section.id('install'))

### [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

| Package                                                        | For                                                 |
| -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| [`yuku-parser`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-parser)     | Parsing to an ESTree / TypeScript-ESTree AST        |
| [`yuku-analyzer`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-analyzer) | Scopes, bindings, references, and module linking    |
| [`yuku-codegen`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-codegen)   | Printing an AST back to source, with source maps    |
| [`yuku-ast`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-ast)           | Walking, building, and checking AST nodes           |

Each package documents its API in its README. The parser and analyzer run on a native binary for each platform.

### [WebAssembly]($section.id('webassembly'))

In browsers, edge runtimes, and on platforms without a native binary, the packages run on [`@yuku-engine/wasm`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@yuku-engine/wasm), with the same API and byte-identical results. Load it once, and every package uses it.

```js
import { init } from "@yuku-engine/wasm";
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

await init();
parse("const x = 1;");
```

Runtimes that can read files load it on first use, without `init`. Where they can `require()` ES modules, the packages also fall back to it when the native binary is missing.

### [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```bash
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku.git
```

```zig
// build.zig
const yuku = b.dependency("yuku", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
my_module.addImport("parser", yuku.module("parser"));
```

Yuku requires Zig 0.16.0 or later.

## [Current status]($section.id('current-status'))

The parser is stable and production ready, from Zig and from JavaScript. The [semantic model](/parser/semantic/) and [traverser](/parser/traverse/) are stable and power Yuku's semantic checker.

## [Performance]($section.id('performance'))

Parsing natively, Yuku is faster than Oxc and roughly 2× faster than SWC.

![Parsing `typescript.js` (7.8 MB) · macOS (ARM), Apple M3, 16 GB · [benchmark source](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-native)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-native/refs/heads/main/charts/typescript.png "Native benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, and other Zig/Rust parsers parsing typescript.js")

On npm, Yuku is **3-10x faster** than alternatives.

![Parsing `react.js` (72 KB) via npm · macOS (ARM), Apple M3, 16 GB · [benchmark source](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/react.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, Acorn, and other parsers parsing react.js")

The lead holds on large files.

![Parsing `typescript.js` (7.8 MB) via npm · macOS (ARM), Apple M3, 16 GB · [benchmark source](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/typescript.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, Acorn, and other parsers parsing typescript.js")

The AST crosses from Zig to JavaScript as a compact binary buffer rather than JSON, and every parser is timed until its full AST is in hand. Oxc's npm package defers its `JSON.parse` to the first `program` access, so timing bare `parse` calls would hide that cost.

## [Size]($section.id('size'))

Installed with npm, dependencies included:

| To                            | Yuku                       | Others                                                                                      |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Parse                         | `yuku-parser` **730 kB**   | `oxc-parser` 4.7 MB, `@babel/parser` 4.8 MB, `@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree` 26.3 MB |
| Analyze scopes and references | `yuku-analyzer` **860 kB** | `@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree` + `@typescript-eslint/scope-manager` 26.7 MB         |
| Print an AST                  | `yuku-codegen` **200 kB**  | `astring` 270 kB, `@babel/generator` 2.5 MB                                                 |

The parser and analyzer share one 610 kB native binary, installed once however many packages use it. `yuku-codegen` and `yuku-ast` are plain JavaScript, and the WebAssembly build is installed only where it runs.
